    "narrative": "The Zurich Zebras enter today\u2019s slate with a lineup anchored by two high-efficiency offensive threats. **Yordan Alvarez** (76.23) is our strongest play, leveraging a high xwOBA against Colin Rea and a favorable batting order spot, while **Christian Yelich** (75.84) follows closely; his score is significantly boosted by a dynamic 13% platoon advantage against Roki Sasaki. We are also looking for a major spark on the basepaths from **Trea Turner** (71.06), whose matchup against Slade Cecconi is highlighted by the pitcher\u2019s slow delivery, creating an ideal environment for **Trea Turner** to exploit his elite speed\u2014even with a slight negative adjustment for wind interference. Meanwhile, **Pete Crow-Armstrong** (65.81) earns a spot in the outfield, as the optimizer heavily favored his 14% platoon edge despite an suboptimal batting order placement.\n\nWe faced a difficult choice regarding the final utility spot, with **Casey Schmitt** (50.65) narrowly missing the cut. While **Casey Schmitt** offered some upside against Bryan Hudson, the lack of a firm lineup confirmation and a negative platoon differential ultimately forced him to the bench in favor of more stable production. We are closely monitoring the status of **Gary Sanchez**, **Dillon Dingler**, **Colt Keith**, **Caleb Durbin**, **Chase Meidroth**, **Kazuma Okamoto**, **Kyle Schwarber**, **Carson Benge**, and **Luke Raley**, as all are currently pending official lineup confirmation. **Kazuma Okamoto** faces the most significant hurdle of the day, as he must contend with the elite arm of Paul Skenes (2.96 xERA), which suppresses his overall projection to a marginal 43.02. Beyond the active roster, we continue to stash **Junior Caminero**, **Hyeseong Kim**, **Zac Veen**, **Lars Nootbaar**, **Josh Smith**, **Luis Pena**, and **Pedro Ramirez** as we await further roster clarity and improved matchups.",
